    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ee31a900_d8a636d7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003247
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-11T15:55:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I know you\u0027ll hate me for this... but when I mentioned this patchset to Lorenzo he pointed out that iterating through the entire bpf map from jni *might* well be faster than this theoretically optimized code path in java...\n(the bpf map is probably not actually all that large...)\n\nThus, it may make sense to keep all this map caching logic constrained to C++...\n(ie. put the name-\u003eifindex cache inside the jni)\n\nWe could also consider whether switching to bpf map bulk reads/dumps wouldn\u0027t be more worthwhile (requires a new enough kernel, and we don\u0027t have any helpers for it yet) - as that would get rid of a fair number of bpf() system calls, or somehow teaching whatever code is performance critical to only ever query a single ifindex (perhaps go via ifname -\u003e ifindex, and query just the currently active one, and just ignore the results if the ifindex has changed since the last query...)\n\nDo you have any benchmark numbers for this change?\n\n[I\u0027ve also not looked too deeply at the code, but at first glance, it would be nice to keep the lock hidden to the inside of the class, though no idea how to do that]",
      "revId": "2b30583f9aaf9795612db23d6d36056d85ae2c4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4cefe3f5_daf6284f",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1952030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-11T22:12:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "85% of bpfGetIfaceStats\u0027s time is spent in BPF syscalls. Doing the same thing from JNI won\u0027t save us much. One of the issues is that, sometimes the map really is that large. I see devices with \u003e150 interfaces, most of which are ipsec.\n\nI don\u0027t necessarily think iterating the map is the issue, it\u0027s that we iterate the map for each interface we want to query. If you want to query all 150 interfaces, this is O(n^2) and you\u0027re looking at 22,500 syscalls (really 2x that since most clients want both rx and tx). Having a hashmap that allows O(1) lookup to find the (likely small) number of ifindexes making the aforementioned case O(N).\n\nI have no issue with keeping the cache in c++. Using the bulk query API is also an option, but truthfully I still think this cache would be more performant and the bulk API is only on new kernels anyways.",
      "parentUuid": "ee31a900_d8a636d7",
      "revId": "2b30583f9aaf9795612db23d6d36056d85ae2c4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9171355a_b3df7eda",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003247
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-12T10:58:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looking at the jni code we seem to do 2 or 3 bpf system calls per map entry.\n\nThe map lookup in getIfaceNameFromMap() should be easiest to get rid of,\nas it should be possible to simply cache it...\n\n(that map should be write once, with no overwrites)\n\nFully caching this would also require us to cache negative lookups, which would be annoying but also possible to do: it would need to be time based, but reading current time does not require a system call...\n\nThat would get us down to 1 or 2 bpf system calls per map entry.\n\nThen we should probably switch from iterate to iterateWithValue, while also improving it to use BPF_MAP_LOOKUP_BATCH on new enough kernels.\n\n--\n\nOr maybe there\u0027s some way to fix this differently?\nIt feels like always-on-vpn/ppn is really screwing us...\nMaybe we need to shrink this map to only store active interfaces?\nHmm...\n\n--\n\nAlternatively we could perhaps replace the entire lookup with\na full map dump+parse+cache and then just return results directly from cache\nif there\u0027s been a dump/parse/cache in the last 0.1 seconds...\n\nThe java APIs are *ridiculously* inefficient...\nWe get all 4 (rx/tx packets/bytes) at the same time,\nbut java can only return 1 so most things probably call us 2 or even 4 times in quick succession...",
      "revId": "2b30583f9aaf9795612db23d6d36056d85ae2c4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "98776c9d_fa52047b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1757736
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-15T14:39:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure how appropriate it would be to use BPF_MAP_LOOKUP_BATCH, since this would only improve the NetworkStats performance of newer kernels while leaving older ones unimproved. However, removing the map lookup in getIfaceNameFromMap() sounds good to me, and seems like it would roll in nicely with the proposal to generally move the caching logic out of the Java components here.\n\nI don\u0027t think I understand why the cache would need to account for time; this sounds like it would add some extra complexity to a simple cache for storing interface indices for each name. Could you explain a bit more?\n\nAs for the alternative fix (only storing active interfaces)—I think the current logic for iterating through indices for matching iface names is a clear issue. While it would help to reduce the size of the map by only storing active interfaces, I think that\u0027s probably best left as a follow-up optimization after the index-\u003ename inefficiencies have been fixed.",
      "parentUuid": "9171355a_b3df7eda",
      "revId": "2b30583f9aaf9795612db23d6d36056d85ae2c4e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
